      {
        "id": "p16-q49-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Use polynomial long division to find the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $x^2-2x+1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2$.",
          "worked": "Long division gives\n$$\\begin{array}{r|rrrrr}\n& & &x^2&-2x&+1\\\\\\hline\nx^2-2x+1&x^4&-4x^3&+6x^2&-4x&+3\\\\\n&-x^4&+2x^3&-x^2&&\\\\\\hline\n&&-2x^3&+5x^2&-4x&+3\\\\\n&&+2x^3&-4x^2&+2x&\\\\\\hline\n&&&x^2&-2x&+3\\\\\n&&&-x^2&+2x&-1\\\\\\hline\n&&&&&2\n\\end{array}$$\nThe quotient is $x^2-2x+1$ and the remainder is $2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 124
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q49-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Deduce that the remainder is the lowest possible value of the polynomial for any value of $x$, and state the value of $x$ when this occurs.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Minimum $2$, occurring at $x=1$.\n\nUse $P(x)=(x-1)^4+2$ and $(x-1)^4\\geq0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\nP(x)&=(x^2-2x+1)^2+2\\\\\nP(x)&=(x-1)^4+2.\n\\end{align*}$$\nFor every real $x$, $(x-1)^4\\geq0$, with equality at $x=1$. Thus the lowest possible value is $2$, occurring at $x=1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
